            },
            {
                "entry_id": "ddfae3de-e31e-30c8-865f-606e55b944fc",
                "word": "laure",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "696e60a0-cc77-427d-bfd2-44f193332360",
                        "entry_id": "ddfae3de-e31e-30c8-865f-606e55b944fc",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "laures"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "09a7ccaa-9d01-3ca6-9fcb-43579015a1cb",
                        "entry_id": "ddfae3de-e31e-30c8-865f-606e55b944fc",
                        "glosses": [
                            "(Église d’Orient) Habitation de moines solitaires, composée de cellules rangées en rond, séparées les unes des autres, et au milieu desquelles était une église."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "adc27bd3-2bb6-4466-8382-b6c7fbefb58a",
                                "sense_id": "09a7ccaa-9d01-3ca6-9fcb-43579015a1cb",
                                "text": "Saint Gérasime fixa sa demeure dans la Palestine, sur les bords du Jourdain, et fonda une laure très-célèbre."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2fc3c55a-661a-4a00-960e-607e760fe230",
                                "sense_id": "09a7ccaa-9d01-3ca6-9fcb-43579015a1cb",
                                "text": "On appelle laure un grand nombre de cellules éparses ou réunies, qui formaient une espèce de village peuplé par des anachorètes. Les plus célèbres étaient celles de la Thébaïde, du mont Sinaï et des environs de la mer Morte; il y en avait qui renfermaient plusieurs milliers de solitaires. D'après sa racine, ce mot vient de laurus, laurier."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "85081800-e5a9-48a1-baa6-2086e323bf90",
                                "sense_id": "09a7ccaa-9d01-3ca6-9fcb-43579015a1cb",
                                "text": "Telle est la relation que le diacre Georges écrivit dans la laure du mont Athos, à une époque qui peut aller du VIIᵉ au XIVᵉ siècle de l’ère chrétienne ; je flotte à cet égard dans une grande incertitude."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "051bc8ad-dc7f-39c6-9200-0fd72a2db561",
                        "entry_id": "ddfae3de-e31e-30c8-865f-606e55b944fc",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Monastère de l’Église d’Orient."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "911c13bc-a8e0-48f7-ba21-8dcbc6e91aaa",
                                "sense_id": "051bc8ad-dc7f-39c6-9200-0fd72a2db561",
                                "text": "En même temps que la cathédrale, Starov érigea l'église qui surmonte le portail de la laure Alexandre Nevski et qui achève la perspective Nevski."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "bafb9d8c-d193-4100-a8d5-7ca977710cc8",
                                "sense_id": "051bc8ad-dc7f-39c6-9200-0fd72a2db561",
                                "text": "Situé au sud du centre-ville de la capitale ukrainienne, la laure des Grottes de Kyïv, construite au milieu du XIe siècle, est le plus ancien monastère d'Ukraine."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "07fb4bec-1c77-49bf-944e-ebcf3b116924",
                        "source_entry_id": "ddfae3de-e31e-30c8-865f-606e55b944fc",
                        "target_lang_word": "lavra",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "lavra",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "8d2c5611-6316-3483-b1bc-c3250a3252d3",
                                        "entry_id": "055daf01-dd69-316b-bf81-20a658579e22",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Alternative form of laura."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "alternative"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "dcb84c20-d1ee-4b42-9b6d-f020fe336a35",
                                                "sense_id": "8d2c5611-6316-3483-b1bc-c3250a3252d3",
                                                "text": "2000, Edward G. Mathews, Jr., Lavra, entry in William M. Johnston (editor), Encyclopedia of Monasticism: A-L, page 747,\nHe left this and, together with a fellow monk, Theoctistus, founded a number of other monasteries and lavras, including the lavra on the cliff in Wadi Mukallik and another in the area of Mishor Adummim."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5a1b985e-c29a-49b1-a198-5ed49a371aba",
                                                "sense_id": "8d2c5611-6316-3483-b1bc-c3250a3252d3",
                                                "text": "The other 46 signatories of the Tragos can be assumed to have been hegoumenoi of lavras — no doubt perfectly well-run holy houses in their way, but they lacked the architectural grandeur of the Great Lavra, and they lacked its staying power too."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f0f3fecd-dc81-471d-9dd2-e6910325ed22",
                                                "sense_id": "8d2c5611-6316-3483-b1bc-c3250a3252d3",
                                                "text": "Also important were the lavras (colonies of hermits) established in Palestine between the fourth and early sixth centuries, notably those founded by St Euthymius (+473) and his disciple St Sabas (+532)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "8159c00d-e2ab-4b19-914a-b8cb53cb627d",
                        "source_entry_id": "ddfae3de-e31e-30c8-865f-606e55b944fc",
                        "target_lang_word": "laura",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "laura",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "7c804e9f-89fe-347c-946f-551afcd2fff2",
                                        "entry_id": "61b62b7b-570d-3c86-9026-19516cf5ba33",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A number of hermitages or cells in the same neighborhood occupied by anchorites who were under the same superior"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "historical"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "581dc14d-ce73-4c28-a2ee-72dfdc025e08",
                                                "sense_id": "7c804e9f-89fe-347c-946f-551afcd2fff2",
                                                "text": "The solitaries of the Thebaid found that they became selfish wild beasts, or went mad, if they remained alone; and they formed themselves into lauras, 'lanes' of huts, convents, under a common abbot or father."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "dfe0507a-16c0-38f9-b16e-9a2252bc9d49",
                                        "entry_id": "61b62b7b-570d-3c86-9026-19516cf5ba33",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A cluster of cells or caves for hermits, with a church and sometimes a refectory at the centre."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "historical"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "bc2e8102-d6a8-4c32-a1bb-e837ce559f9c",
                                                "sense_id": "dfe0507a-16c0-38f9-b16e-9a2252bc9d49",
                                                "text": "There were the cenobia, or monasteries proper, where the life was according to the lines laid down by St Basil; and there were the lauras, wherein a semi-eremitical life was followed, the monks living in separate huts within the enclosure."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "456a8c0c-5634-4852-97bb-b43097a46aa9",
                        "source_entry_id": "ddfae3de-e31e-30c8-865f-606e55b944fc",
